    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "У нас французский ресторан.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'We have a French restaurant.' Chef Viktor links 'at' directly with 'us' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А не японский.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And not Japanese.' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'Japanese'. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я обожаю суши.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I adore sushi.' Kristina links 'I' directly with 'adore' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я ем их каждый день.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I eat them every day.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'day'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А если ты захочешь хачапури?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what if you want khachapuri?' Notice how 'and' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'khachapuri'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я вызову хачапурщика.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will call a khachapuri maker.' Kristina combines 'I' with 'khachapuri baker' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Senya responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "senya",
      "t": "Я умею готовить хачапури.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I know how to cook khachapuri.' Senya links 'I' directly with 'to know how to' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Заткнись и пошёл вон.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Shut up and get out.' The verb for 'went' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "С тобой невозможно разговаривать.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It is impossible to talk to you.' Chef Viktor combines 'with' with 'to talk' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Кстати, Вика у нас не работает.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'By the way, Vika doesn't work for us.' Kristina combines 'by the way' with 'works' in a natural Russian turn of phrase. Then Chef Viktor responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты её уволила?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Did you fire her?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'to fire'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Она сама уволилась.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
